}", "style": { "fill": "#ffffff", "stroke": "#d9541f", "stroke_width": null, "dash": null, "opacity": null, "corner_radius": null, "font": "mono", "font_size": 13.0, "font_weight": null, "italic": null, "text_color": null, "align": "left", "padding": null } }, { "type": "label", "id": "n1", "x": 48.0, "y": 392.0, "text": "The interface vanished entirely: an interface is a compile-time shape, not an object.
So you cannot test a type with typeof or instanceof, and data crossing the boundary
(JSON from a server, a form field, localStorage) is NOT checked. Validate it yourself.", "w": 886.0, "style": { "fill": null, "stroke": null, "stroke_width": null, "dash": null, "opacity": null, "corner_radius": null, "font": null, "font_size": 13.0, "font_weight": null, "italic": null, "text_color": "#6b6a66", "align": "left", "padding": null } }, { "type": "shape", "id": "why", "kind": "note", "x": 48.0, "y": 462.0, "w": 886.0, "h": 104.0, "text": "Why bother, then? Because the check happens BEFORE you ship: a typo, a wrong argument order, or a forgotten null is a red
squiggle in the editor instead of a bug in production. TypeScript is a superset: every valid .js file is already valid .ts.
In this course we run .ts straight through tsx, which strips the types without type-checking; tsc --noEmit does the checking.", "style": { "fill": "#fbeecf", "stroke": "#b07d00", "stroke_width": null, "dash": null, "opacity": null, "corner_radius": null, "font": null, "font_size": 13.0, "font_weight": null, "italic": null, "text_color": "#5c4100", "align": "left", "padding": null } } ], "next_id": { "conn": 3 } }
